# Contributing

Thanks for considering a contribution to Specs Agent Platform. This document
covers the contributor workflow.

## Getting set up

The workspace is managed with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/).

```bash
uv sync --all-packages
```

This installs all three packages (`open-agent-core`, `open-agent-lang`,
`open-agent-adk`) plus the shared dev dependency group (pytest, ruff, mypy).

## Before opening a pull request

Run the same checks CI runs, from the repository root:

```bash
uv run --all-packages ruff check .
uv run --all-packages ruff format --check .
uv run --all-packages mypy tests packages/open-agent-core/src packages/open-agent-lang/src packages/open-agent-adk/src
uv run --all-packages pytest --cov --cov-report=term-missing --cov-report=xml --cov-fail-under=70
uv run --all-packages python scripts/generate_openapi.py --check
uv build --all-packages
```

If you have [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) installed, `pre-commit run
--all-files` runs the lint/format/type-check subset automatically before each
commit — see `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.

When a runner route or request/response model changes, regenerate the checked-in
API documents with:

```bash
uv run --all-packages python scripts/generate_openapi.py
```

The `--check` form used by CI fails when either generated document has drifted.

Docker image builds and Compose profile smoke tests also run in CI. They are
opt-in locally:

```bash
RUN_DOCKER_INTEGRATION=1 uv run --all-packages pytest
```

Pull requests additionally build and vulnerability-scan both engine images,
but never publish an image or receive package-write credentials. Do not add a
registry token to pull-request workflows. Release tags and manual releases use
the repository `GITHUB_TOKEN` with narrowly scoped package permissions; OCI
provenance and SBOM attestations are generated by BuildKit. See [release
operations](docs/releasing.md).

## Making changes

- Keep `open-agent-core` framework-neutral. The `open-agent-lang` and
  `open-agent-adk` packages depend on core, never the reverse.
- Changes that touch shared contracts (the IR, the loader, the runtime,
  persistence) must be validated against both engine packages.
- Add or update tests under `tests/` for any behavior change. New modules
  should have corresponding unit tests rather than relying only on the
  conformance fixtures.
- Add a conformance fixture in `tests/fixtures/conformance/` when you add
  support for a new Open Workflow v1 shape or Agent Spec component type.
  Workflow fixtures go in `workflows/`, agent fixtures in `agents/`, and the
  existing `bindings.yaml` resolves shared bindings. The parametrized tests in
  `tests/test_conformance.py` automatically pick up new fixtures.
- Update `README.md` and `TODO.md` when a change affects documented behavior
  or completes a tracked roadmap item.
- For a release, keep `pyproject.toml`, `CHANGELOG.md`, and the `vX.Y.Z` Git
  tag aligned, and follow the [release checklist](docs/releasing.md). Do not
  reuse a full version tag.

## Workspace hygiene

- Keep credentials in an untracked `.env` file or a secret manager. Commit only
  the safe variable template in `.env.example`.
- Do not commit virtual environments, Python/tool caches, coverage output,
  package builds, local checkpoint databases, logs, Trivy data, or exported
  container archives. The root `.gitignore` covers these generated paths.
- Keep `docs/openapi-lang.json` and `docs/openapi-adk.json` tracked; they are
  generated release documentation, not disposable build output.
- The root `.dockerignore` deliberately omits tests, contributor/operations
  docs, automation metadata, local state, and secrets from runtime build
  contexts. It keeps package sources, examples, lockfiles, license metadata,
  and the root README required by package metadata.
- Before committing, review `git status --short` and `git diff --check`. Remove
  generated files only when their exact path and purpose are known.

## Commit and PR expectations

- Keep commits focused; prefer several small, reviewable commits over one
  large one.
- Describe the "why" in the PR description, not just the "what" — link the
  relevant `TODO.md` item or issue if there is one.
- Ensure CI is green before requesting review.

## Reporting bugs and security issues

Use GitHub issues for bugs and feature requests. For security
vulnerabilities, follow the process in [`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md) instead
of filing a public issue.
